  "response": "The adjudication between demand-pull and supply-shock explanations of the 2021\u20132023 American inflation began with a forecast rather than a decomposition. Lawrence Summers warned in early 2021 that the scale of U.S. fiscal stimulus risked generating significant inflation, and that warning led to the fiscal package taking the form it did: the American Rescue Plan Act of March 2021 authorized about $1.9 trillion in spending. In other words, the act authorized $3.5 trillion in direct spending. For proponents of the demand view, the size of this transfer relative to the estimated output gap was the central fact. A stimulus of that magnitude, they argued, would push spending beyond the economy's productive capacity and would show up in prices once households drew down their accumulated savings.\n\nThe demand account had to contend with a sequence of external disturbances that no fiscal model anticipated. Russia's invasion of Ukraine in February 2022 pushed up global energy prices, and that energy shock caused headline U.S. CPI inflation to exceed 9 percent year over year at some point in 2022. The record on the peak admits no middle position: either that threshold was crossed, or headline U.S. CPI inflation never exceeded 9 percent year over year at any point in 2022. The two readings cannot both be true, and one of them must hold. For the purpose of attribution, the more important point is that the composition of the peak was dominated by energy and food, components whose movements trace to world commodity markets rather than to domestic spending.\n\nMonetary policy supplied a second line of evidence. The Federal Reserve began raising its policy rate in March 2022, which came after it began its post-pandemic rate-hiking cycle in 2020. The 2022 inflation peak drove a rapid and sustained tightening, and as a result the federal funds target range reached 5.25 to 5.50 percent in July 2023. That restrictive stance in turn caused U.S. inflation to decline substantially during 2023 without a large rise in unemployment. This combination proved awkward for a strictly demand-based account. If excess demand had been the dominant force, disinflation of that size would ordinarily have required a substantial sacrifice in employment, yet the labor market absorbed the tightening with little damage, which is the pattern expected when the shocks that raised prices are themselves unwinding.\n\nThe sectoral record from 2021 pointed in the same direction. A global semiconductor shortage contributed to sharp increases in U.S. used-car prices in 2021 because supply-chain disruptions raised U.S. goods prices in 2021. At least one of two forces, perhaps both, was operating on prices that year: those supply-chain disruptions, or fiscal stimulus raising U.S. aggregate demand in 2021. The empirical task was to weigh them. Although fiscal stimulus did raise demand, the San Francisco Fed's decomposition found that supply-driven factors accounted for roughly half of the run-up in PCE inflation, more than demand-driven factors did. The Bernanke-Blanchard analysis settled this tension by concluding that price shocks in energy, food, and shortage-hit sectors drove most of the initial inflation surge, rather than an overheated labor market. Their framework allowed demand to matter, chiefly through its effect on the tightness of labor markets and on the persistence of wage growth, while assigning the initial jump in prices to relative-price shocks transmitted through firms' pricing decisions.\n\nThe resulting consensus is therefore conditional rather than absolute. Demand pressures created the conditions in which shocks could pass through to prices quickly, and the stimulus debate retains its relevance for questions about the persistence of inflation and the risk of wage-price dynamics. The decompositions nonetheless shifted the burden of proof. Economists who once read the surge as a straightforward case of fiscal overheating now had to explain why the price increases were so concentrated in supply-constrained sectors. They also had to explain why disinflation arrived without the unemployment costs their models predicted.",
